  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nA novel approach based on **Gaussian Process Optimization** with integrated **local search heuristics** is proposed to tackle the minimal overlap problem. This differs fundamentally from gradient-based optimization by using Bayesian optimization over continuous state spaces, allowing us to:\n- Exploit the entire solution space without being trapped in local optima\n- Gracefully handle the non-convex, multimodal nature of the problem\n- Adaptively determine promising query points based on uncertainty estimates\n\nThe key bottleneck addressed here is the ill-conditioned optimization landscape of the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem, where traditional gradient methods often fail near optimal solutions. By combining global exploration with targeted local exploitation, we overcome this challenge.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe problem is formulated as:\nMinimize $C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx$\nSubject to:\n$\\frac{1}{2} \\int_0^2 h(x) dx = 1$, $0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1$\n\nDiscrete representation: piecewise constant function over $n$ points with $dx=2/n$\n\nProbability model:\nEvaluate the posterior distribution of the objective as a function of smoothness parameter $\\alpha$ and correlation length $\\ell$\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na. Initialize with parent from `initial_h_values` at 640 points\nb. Two-phase optimization:\n  i. Global phase: Gaussian Process with Mat\u00e9rn kernel\n  ii. Local phase: Gradient descent with adaptive sampling\nc. Hyperparameter scheduling:\n   $\\alpha_0 = 60, \\ell_0 = 20, \\nu=5/2$\n   Exponential decay: $\\alpha \\propto e^{-t/\\theta}$, $\\ell \\propto e^{-t/\\phi}$\n\nd. Represent the optimization space as:\n    $X \\in [0,1]^{n} \\times \\mathbb{R}^{+}$\n\ne. Error handling: automatic repair via projection operators\nf. Budget allocation: 40% wall-clock to global, 60% to local\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nDefault configuration:\n- $\\tau = 100$ total budget in seconds\n- $N_{points} = 256$ base resolution\n- $\\alpha_max = 8000, \\ell_max = 500$\n\nAdaptive adjustments:\n- If plateau detected, increase $\\ell$ by 20% and decrease $\\nu$\n- If divergence, reset $\\alpha$ to $initial_\\alpha$\n- If improvement below threshold, increase $N_{points}$ by doubling\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nDominant cost: $O(n^2)$ per kernel evaluation\nTime estimation: $t(n) \\approx 1.5 n^2$ operations per budget factor\nWall-clock monitoring: check every 5 seconds\nFallback procedure: keep best candidate when optimization stalls\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nAll candidates must satisfy:\n- $0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\forall i$\n- $|\\sum h[i] \\cdot dx - 1| < 1e-6$\n- Sanitary checks for correlation calculation\n- Actual Erd\u0151s C5 evaluation with:\n  $C5 = \\frac{\\max(\\text{np.correlate}(h,1-h,\\text{mode}='full')}{n} \\cdot dx$\n\nReturn format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
